1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,891
Can a finite list of repeating number
patterns catch every integer?

2
00:00:04,891 --> 00:00:07,738
Start with twelve positions on a clock.

3
00:00:07,738 --> 00:00:11,826
Colour every even position. Then add
multiples of three.

4
00:00:11,826 --> 00:00:17,520
Some positions remain empty. We can keep
adding remainder rules until none do.

5
00:00:18,067 --> 00:00:22,798
Next take numbers leaving remainder one on
division by four, then remainder

6
00:00:22,798 --> 00:00:27,089
five on division by six, then remainder
seven on division by twelve.

7
00:00:27,089 --> 00:00:31,947
Every clock position is covered. Because
all five divisors divide twelve, the

8
00:00:31,947 --> 00:00:34,723
picture repeats along the whole number
line.

9
00:00:35,267 --> 00:00:39,608
This is a covering system. The divisor in
each rule is its modulus.

10
00:00:39,608 --> 00:00:43,820
Our moduli are two, three, four, six, and
twelve, each used once.

11
00:00:43,820 --> 00:00:48,227
Distinct means we cannot use the same
modulus again with a different

12
00:00:48,227 --> 00:00:48,875
remainder.

13
00:00:49,400 --> 00:00:53,912
Now impose a harder restriction. Every
modulus must be at least six, and

14
00:00:53,912 --> 00:00:56,543
divide forty-five times some power of two.

15
00:00:56,543 --> 00:01:01,118
We may choose the power as large as we
like, but the list must be finite.

16
00:01:01,118 --> 00:01:03,248
Can all integers still be covered?

17
00:01:03,767 --> 00:01:06,417
The answer is no, for every power of two.

18
00:01:06,417 --> 00:01:10,102
The proof does not search through
infinitely many powers.

19
00:01:10,102 --> 00:01:14,563
It checks a small initial stage exactly,
then proves that a numerical

20
00:01:14,563 --> 00:01:17,279
obstruction survives every later doubling.

21
00:01:17,797 --> 00:01:21,601
First separate the odd part from the powers
of two.

22
00:01:21,601 --> 00:01:26,896
The odd divisors of forty-five are one,
three, five, nine, fifteen, and

23
00:01:26,896 --> 00:01:32,639
forty-five. Every permitted modulus has one
of these odd parts, multiplied by

24
00:01:32,639 --> 00:01:33,757
a power of two.

25
00:01:34,297 --> 00:01:39,512
Represent a remainder modulo forty-five by
two coordinates: its remainder

26
00:01:39,512 --> 00:01:42,583
modulo nine, and its remainder modulo five.

27
00:01:42,583 --> 00:01:45,226
There are nine columns and five rows.

28
00:01:45,226 --> 00:01:49,226
For example, seventeen sits in column eight
and row two.

29
00:01:49,226 --> 00:01:52,297
Every integer residue has exactly one cell.

30
00:01:52,831 --> 00:01:55,752
Each odd divisor now gives a shape of
cells.

31
00:01:55,752 --> 00:01:58,143
A rule modulo nine fixes one column.

32
00:01:58,143 --> 00:02:02,990
A rule modulo five fixes one row. A rule
modulo forty-five fixes a single

33
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:07,639
cell. These are repeating arithmetic rules
drawn as pieces on a board.

34
00:02:08,164 --> 00:02:13,236
A rule modulo three selects three spaced
columns, all with the same remainder

35
00:02:13,236 --> 00:02:17,978
modulo three. Modulo fifteen selects those
three columns inside one row.

36
00:02:17,978 --> 00:02:20,284
Modulo one selects the whole board.

37
00:02:20,284 --> 00:02:23,116
These six types are all the pieces we need.

38
00:02:23,661 --> 00:02:28,494
Next track parity. One board splits into
two: an even branch and an odd

39
00:02:28,494 --> 00:02:32,510
branch. Modulo four, each splits again,
giving four boards.

40
00:02:32,510 --> 00:02:37,819
Knowing the remainder modulo four and
modulo forty-five identifies a remainder

41
00:02:37,819 --> 00:02:39,861
modulo one hundred and eighty.

42
00:02:40,394 --> 00:02:45,134
A rule whose modulus is two to the power i
times an odd divisor chooses a

43
00:02:45,134 --> 00:02:48,446
branch at depth i, and a piece type on that
branch.

44
00:02:48,446 --> 00:02:52,212
Below that branch it keeps covering the
same odd residues.

45
00:02:52,212 --> 00:02:55,394
Existing patterns are inherited by both
children.

46
00:02:55,927 --> 00:03:00,777
At each new depth, there is at most one new
rule of each of the six types.

47
00:03:00,777 --> 00:03:05,561
Why? Reusing a type at the same depth would
reuse the exact same modulus.

48
00:03:05,561 --> 00:03:10,279
Distinctness limits the new supply of
pieces, even as the boards double.

49
00:03:10,826 --> 00:03:14,138
Counting uncovered cells alone is not
enough.

50
00:03:14,138 --> 00:03:19,659
Their arrangement matters. Instead, assign
prices to pieces: fourteen for a

51
00:03:19,659 --> 00:03:25,401
whole board, seven for a three-column
block, four for a row, two for a column,

52
00:03:25,401 --> 00:03:28,346
two for a short row, and one for a cell.

53
00:03:28,892 --> 00:03:33,436
A board's cost is the cheapest total price
of pieces covering its remaining

54
00:03:33,436 --> 00:03:37,859
cells. In this bookkeeping calculation,
repeated piece types are allowed.

55
00:03:37,859 --> 00:03:42,342
That is a relaxed measuring tool; it does
not allow repeated moduli in the

56
00:03:42,342 --> 00:03:43,796
actual covering problem.

57
00:03:44,326 --> 00:03:48,080
Here is a real uncovered pattern from the
retained check.

58
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:53,086
It contains seventeen cells. Four columns
cover them all, at price two each,

59
00:03:53,086 --> 00:03:58,224
for cost eight. The exact cost routine also
checks that no cheaper combination

60
00:03:58,224 --> 00:03:59,542
of pieces can do it.

61
00:04:00,059 --> 00:04:03,096
Suppose a new rule removes the highlighted
column.

62
00:04:03,096 --> 00:04:06,315
Cover the remaining cells, then buy that
column back.

63
00:04:06,315 --> 00:04:08,805
You have a cover of the original pattern.

64
00:04:08,805 --> 00:04:13,542
So removing a piece can lower the minimum
cost by no more than the piece's own

65
00:04:13,542 --> 00:04:13,907
price.

66
00:04:14,424 --> 00:04:18,586
Add the minimum costs of all current boards
and call the total Q.

67
00:04:18,586 --> 00:04:22,428
Before any new rules, doubling the boards
doubles Q exactly.

68
00:04:22,428 --> 00:04:27,039
The children inherit identical uncovered
patterns, so each parent's cost

69
00:04:27,039 --> 00:04:27,936
appears twice.

70
00:04:28,457 --> 00:04:32,749
Now spend the new rules. There is at most
one piece of each type.

71
00:04:32,749 --> 00:04:35,654
Their prices add to thirty, which we call
R.

72
00:04:35,654 --> 00:04:39,616
Together they can reduce the doubled cost
by at most thirty.

73
00:04:39,616 --> 00:04:42,257
So the next Q is at least two Q minus R.

74
00:04:42,790 --> 00:04:47,437
Here is the trap. If Q has already reached
thirty, then the next value is at

75
00:04:47,437 --> 00:04:50,495
least two times thirty minus thirty: still
thirty.

76
00:04:50,495 --> 00:04:53,002
Repeat the argument at every later depth.

77
00:04:53,002 --> 00:04:54,958
The cost can never fall to zero.

78
00:04:55,490 --> 00:05:00,270
A completed covering has no uncovered
cells, so every board costs zero.

79
00:05:00,270 --> 00:05:04,511
Therefore, once Q reaches R, no finite
continuation can finish.

80
00:05:04,511 --> 00:05:09,492
That is the step turning a calculation at
one small depth into a statement

81
00:05:09,492 --> 00:05:12,050
about arbitrarily large powers of two.

82
00:05:12,588 --> 00:05:17,076
The remaining job is finite. At depth two
there are four boards and thirteen

83
00:05:17,076 --> 00:05:21,150
allowed moduli, all dividing one hundred
and eighty and at least six.

84
00:05:21,150 --> 00:05:25,519
Check every possible placement of those
remainder rules, including choices

85
00:05:25,519 --> 00:05:26,700
that omit a modulus.

86
00:05:27,222 --> 00:05:30,649
One price list is not enough for the
retained proof.

87
00:05:30,649 --> 00:05:35,526
There are eleven lists. For every initial
configuration, at least one list

88
00:05:35,526 --> 00:05:39,745
reaches its own threshold. The list that
works can depend on the

89
00:05:39,745 --> 00:05:44,358
configuration. After choosing it, the same
doubling inequality applies

90
00:05:44,358 --> 00:05:44,886
forever.

91
00:05:45,422 --> 00:05:49,107
These four exact boards show why the
eleventh list matters.

92
00:05:49,107 --> 00:05:53,791
Their uncovered cell counts are zero,
seventeen, eighteen, and twenty-four.

93
00:05:53,791 --> 00:05:55,915
They survived the first ten tests.

94
00:05:55,915 --> 00:06:00,038
Now apply the prices we have been using to
the same four patterns.

95
00:06:00,555 --> 00:06:06,531
The empty board costs zero. The second
costs eight, using four columns.

96
00:06:06,531 --> 00:06:09,981
The third costs twelve, using three rows.

97
00:06:09,981 --> 00:06:13,600
The fourth costs twelve, using six columns.

98
00:06:13,600 --> 00:06:19,155
The exact minima add to thirty-two, above
the threshold of thirty.

99
00:06:19,688 --> 00:06:23,576
Doubling this state therefore leaves cost
at least thirty-four.

100
00:06:23,576 --> 00:06:26,353
Double again and it is at least
thirty-eight.

101
00:06:26,353 --> 00:06:30,859
The obstruction persists. With another
price list the same state can fall

102
00:06:30,859 --> 00:06:35,240
below its threshold, so multiple ways of
measuring are doing real work.

103
00:06:35,788 --> 00:06:40,989
The main C search visits four hundred and
ninety-eight thousand, four hundred

104
00:06:40,989 --> 00:06:43,825
and sixty-four nodes, with zero survivors.

105
00:06:43,825 --> 00:06:47,337
A separate generic C implementation also
finds zero.

106
00:06:47,337 --> 00:06:52,267
Independent Python covers one hundred and
thirty-five roots and over four

107
00:06:52,267 --> 00:06:55,036
million nodes, again with zero survivors.

108
00:06:55,555 --> 00:07:00,380
The proof combines those exact finite
checks with the written doubling lemma.

109
00:07:00,380 --> 00:07:03,326
It is not a test of just a few large
exponents.

110
00:07:03,326 --> 00:07:07,963
Controls check known coverings and the cost
calculations, and deliberately

111
00:07:07,963 --> 00:07:10,532
incomplete result summaries are rejected.

112
00:07:10,532 --> 00:07:12,475
There is no Lean formalisation.

113
00:07:13,017 --> 00:07:17,517
There is a surprising limit to the
statement: an infinite distinct covering

114
00:07:17,517 --> 00:07:19,557
with these kinds of moduli exists.

115
00:07:19,557 --> 00:07:21,777
That does not contradict the theorem.

116
00:07:21,777 --> 00:07:25,917
At every finite stage, something remains
uncovered, even though every

117
00:07:25,917 --> 00:07:28,977
particular integer can eventually be
covered later.

118
00:07:29,517 --> 00:07:32,815
Think carefully about the order of those
statements.

119
00:07:32,815 --> 00:07:36,049
For each finite list, there is some missed
integer.

120
00:07:36,049 --> 00:07:40,933
That is compatible with each integer
appearing somewhere in an infinite list.

121
00:07:40,933 --> 00:07:44,421
There need not be one integer that escapes
every stage.

122
00:07:44,950 --> 00:07:49,627
Harrington, Klein, Lowrance and Trifonov
posed this finite question and

123
00:07:49,627 --> 00:07:51,932
supplied the infinite construction.

124
00:07:51,932 --> 00:07:54,435
This result settles their Problem Two.

125
00:07:54,435 --> 00:07:59,177
It does not settle the separate
minimum-eight problem or the exploratory

126
00:07:59,177 --> 00:07:59,440
case

127
00:07:59,440 --> 00:08:02,206
with odd part one hundred and thirty-five.

128
00:08:02,750 --> 00:08:05,273
The conclusion uses every restriction.

129
00:08:05,273 --> 00:08:07,929
The list is finite. Moduli are distinct.

130
00:08:07,929 --> 00:08:12,510
Each is at least six. And all divide
forty-five times a power of two.

131
00:08:12,510 --> 00:08:17,689
Under those conditions, every attempted
covering leaves a gap, however far the

132
00:08:17,689 --> 00:08:18,950
power of two grows.

133
00:08:19,484 --> 00:08:22,952
The central idea is a price that survives
doubling.

134
00:08:22,952 --> 00:08:28,256
Exact enumeration proves every starting
arrangement gets trapped by some price

135
00:08:28,256 --> 00:08:31,384
list. The inequality keeps it trapped
forever.

136
00:08:31,384 --> 00:08:36,688
That is how a finite calculation can prove
an infinite family of impossibility

137
00:08:36,688 --> 00:08:37,436
statements.

138
00:08:37,984 --> 00:08:43,346
The source paper, eleven price vectors,
recorded checks and editable animation

139
00:08:43,346 --> 00:08:48,570
accompany this video. Correctness
confidence is high within the stated proof

140
00:08:48,570 --> 00:08:52,419
and computational model; the priority
search is bounded.

141
00:08:52,419 --> 00:08:54,688
Narration uses a synthetic voice.
